Fix TTR-expired jobs that stay reserved forever (issue #76; upstream bug,
unfixed in v1.13 and on master).

nanoseconds() has microsecond resolution (gettimeofday), and for a connection
that is not waiting, connsched() sets tickat to the reserved job's deadline.
In the final millisecond before the deadline the main loop busy-spins, so
prottick() can find tickat == now and call conn_timeout() at the exact
deadline. prottick() has already removed the connection from the heap, but
conn_timeout() skipped the job because deadline_at >= nanoseconds() still held
within the same microsecond. It then returned without calling connsched(),
so the connection was never scheduled again and the job stayed reserved, with
a negative time-left, until the worker sent another command or disconnected.

Treat a job as expired once its deadline is reached, which matches
prottick(), where only d > 0 means "not due yet".

--- a/prot.c
+++ b/prot.c
@@ -1870,7 +1870,7 @@
     /* Check if any reserved jobs have run out of time. We should do this
      * whether or not the client is waiting for a new reservation. */
     while ((j = connsoonestjob(c))) {
-        if (j->r.deadline_at >= nanoseconds())
+        if (j->r.deadline_at > nanoseconds())
             break;
 
